KG42.register("rules-5ca5234861a4e1ad3c7cfe6f",{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R003","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Market payment","process_stage":"settlement","market_meaning":"The optimal local flexibility-allocation instructions feed compensation calculation: other aggregators' declared costs are evaluated at their instructions for the maximum-cost penalty, and the recipient's implemented flexibility is compared with its instruction for the deviation penalty. Measured consumption and proof of payments also enter the calculation. Scope: Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. The DSO designs the aggregator compensation function and chooses its penalization factor beta. Scope: DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. ECPM includes a fixed payment to each aggregator intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff. Scope: Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. ECPM compensation includes reimbursement of the aggregator's actual flexibility cost as a component, alongside its penalty deductions. Scope: Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. The deviation penalty is quadratic in deviation volume and applies in both upward and downward directions. Its per-unit penalization increases with deviation magnitude, strongly penalizing large deviations while keeping small, possibly unintended deviations mild. Scope: Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction. Condition: An aggregator implements flexibility that deviates from the DSO instruction. Deviation penalties must be high enough to deter misreporting while avoiding levels that make aggregators with uncertainty prefer to opt out because of potential negative payoffs and heavy penalties. Scope: ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function settlement.payment under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["clearing.local","coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E001","source":"clearing.local","target":"settlement.payment","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E002","source":"coordination.local_operator","target":"settlement.payment","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. | DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. | ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty. | Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. | Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. | Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E21"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"var_beta","symbol":"\\beta","meaning":"Penalization factor in the compensation-and-penalty payment rule, chosen by the DSO.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; DSO-chosen scalar penalization factor","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E16"]},{"variable_id":"var_gamma","symbol":"\\gamma","meaning":"Per-unit coefficient of the quadratic deviation penalty, adjustable by the DSO.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; DSO-chosen scalar satisfying the stated incentive and participation trade-off","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E25","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-E21"]},{"variable_id":"var_R_i","symbol":"R_i","meaning":"Fixed payment to aggregator i in ECPM, intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; fixed payment amount for aggregator i","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E21"]},{"variable_id":"var_tildeK_i","symbol":"\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i","meaning":"Actual final flexibility profile implemented by aggregator i and measured by the DSO, possibly differing from the instruction.","kind":"operational","domain":"profile over t in \\Omega_T of measured actual flexibility factors; source does not state explicit numerical bounds for the measured profile","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E16"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"par_K_i_star","symbol":"\\mathbf K_i^*","meaning":"Optimal flexibility instruction communicated to aggregator i from the clearing problem using the declared tuples.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"clearing instruction"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E06"]},{"parameter_id":"par_tilde_c_i","symbol":"\\tilde c_i","meaning":"Observed actual flexibility cost of aggregator i after the horizon, used as the actual-cost reimbursement term and in the maximum-cost penalty.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"observed actual cost"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g03-E15"]},{"parameter_id":"par_L_i","symbol":"\\mathbf L_i","meaning":"Actual local parameter tuple of aggregator i, containing the flexibility cost parameter and lower/upper flexibility limits.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"actual local parameter tuple"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g01-E16"]},{"parameter_id":"par_hatL_j","symbol":"\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j","meaning":"Declared parameter tuple of another aggregator j, used in the maximum-cost penalty term.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"declared parameter tuple of aggregator j"},"unit":null,"indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E06"]},{"parameter_id":"par_c_j_eval","symbol":"c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)","meaning":"Declared flexibility cost of another aggregator j evaluated at its instruction and declared tuple in the maximum-cost penalty.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"declared cost evaluated at instruction"},"unit":null,"indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E11"]},{"parameter_id":"par_Omega_b","symbol":"\\Omega_b","meaning":"Set of nodes/aggregators over which the maximum-cost penalty and sums are indexed.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of nodes/aggregators"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E31"]},{"parameter_id":"par_Omega_T","symbol":"\\Omega_T","meaning":"Set of time periods over which the quadratic deviation penalty is summed.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of time periods"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E31"]},{"parameter_id":"par_lambda_i_t","symbol":"\\lambda_{i,t}","meaning":"Flexibility cost parameter of aggregator i at period t, entering the cost function and the gamma-dominant condition.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"flexibility cost parameter"},"unit":"$/kWh^2","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g01-E10"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The optimal local flexibility-allocation instructions feed compensation calculation: other aggregators' declared costs are evaluated at their instructions for the maximum-cost penalty, and the recipient's implemented flexibility is compared with its instruction for the deviation penalty. Measured consumption and proof of payments also enter the calculation. Scope: Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The DSO designs the aggregator compensation function and chooses its penalization factor beta. Scope: DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"ECPM includes a fixed payment to each aggregator intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff. Scope: Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"ECPM compensation includes reimbursement of the aggregator's actual flexibility cost as a component, alongside its penalty deductions. Scope: Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The deviation penalty is quadratic in deviation volume and applies in both upward and downward directions. Its per-unit penalization increases with deviation magnitude, strongly penalizing large deviations while keeping small, possibly unintended deviations mild. Scope: Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction. Condition: An aggregator implements flexibility that deviates from the DSO instruction.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Deviation penalties must be high enough to deter misreporting while avoiding levels that make aggregators with uncertainty prefer to opt out because of potential negative payoffs and heavy penalties. Scope: ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E21"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"ECPM payment rule (14): the payment to aggregator i equals the fixed payment R_i plus the observed actual cost, minus beta times the maximum of the observed actual cost and the worst declared cost among other aggregators evaluated at their instructions, minus a quadratic deviation penalty summed over the horizon.","expression":"p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L}) = R_i + \\tilde c_i - \\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\right\\} - \\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_beta","var_gamma","var_R_i","var_tildeK_i"],"parameter_refs":["par_K_i_star","par_tilde_c_i","par_hatL_j","par_c_j_eval","par_Omega_b","par_Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E17"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C008","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"Inherited CPM payment rule (13): the CPM reward equals observed actual cost minus beta times the maximum of the observed actual cost and the maximum declared cost of other aggregators evaluated at their instructions.","expression":"p_i^{CPM} = \\tilde c_i - \\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\right\\}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_beta"],"parameter_refs":["par_tilde_c_i","par_K_i_star","par_hatL_j","par_c_j_eval","par_Omega_b"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-E11","v141-b-g02-E12","v141-b-g03-E07"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C009","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The ECPM deviation-penalty component is the negative sum over the horizon of gamma times the squared difference between the actual implemented flexibility factor and the DSO instruction factor.","expression":"-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_gamma","var_tildeK_i"],"parameter_refs":["par_K_i_star","par_Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E17","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E17"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C010","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The source states the incentive-compatibility condition on the deviation-penalty coefficient as gamma much greater than the aggregator's flexibility cost parameter lambda_i,t.","expression":"\\gamma \\gg \\lambda_{i,t}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_gamma"],"parameter_refs":["par_lambda_i_t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E25","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-E18"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"re The payoff of an aggregator i, deﬁned as πi, is calculated te, as the compensation pi received by","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The task of the DSO in this case is to design the reward function, \\(p_i\\), in such a way that it makes it a dominant strategy for each aggregator to first make truthful declarations and then follow the flexibility instruction, i.e., \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\approx\\mathbf L_i\\) and \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\approx\\mathbf K_i^*\\), respectively. That is, the reward function \\(p(\\cdot)\\) should be incentive compatible in the sense that it aligns the aggregators’ local objectives with the general objective of the DSO. This needs to be done in order to achieve the optimal solution to problem (11), since this solution guarantees the safe operation of the distribution system and fairness for flexibility assets."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN (Remarks and Extensions)","anchor_text":"2254 ECPM provides the DSO with the ability to adjust the devi- ation penalty γ , depending on the s","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"ECPM provides the DSO with the ability to adjust the deviation penalty \\gamma, depending on the system characteristics, so that a good trade-off is achieved between not heavily punishing unintended deviations on one hand and disincentivizing strategic behavior on the other. More generally, the choice of values for parameters \\gamma, R_i and \\beta of ECPM need to be carefully engineered so that the aggregators always benefit from participating in the flexibility market. This means that the penalization for deviations needs to be high enough so as to disincentivize misreporting, but at the same time not so high that aggregators with uncertainties would rather not participate in the market at all, in fear of negative payoffs and heavy penalties."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"2252 PDi,t(1 −Ki,t)), which comes at a cost deﬁned as 2 ci(Ki, Li) = λi,t PDi,t 1 −Ki,t ∀i ∈ b. (8) ","equation_id":"(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"This is modelled by causing a deviation in the prosumers’ active power demand (from \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) to \\(P^D_{i,t}(1-K_{i,t})\\)), which comes at a cost defined as \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)=\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\lambda_{i,t}\\left(P^D_{i,t}(1-K_{i,t})\\right)^2\\quad \\forall i\\in\\Omega_b.\\) (8)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Once the instruction is communicated to each aggregator for the horizon \\(\\Omega_T\\), real-time operation begins. The aggregator implements a flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), where the tilde denotes the fact that this is the actual final flexibility factor implemented by the aggregator, as measured by the DSO’s smart meters. In real time operation, two things may go differently than scheduled: • The aggregator might not follow the instruction, and implement a different final profile, i.e., \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne \\mathbf K_i^*\\). • The actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\) that the aggregator pays to its customers for the implementation of the flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), may not be equal to the cost calculated by the DSO, \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\), e.g., aggregators providing false declarations of flexibility costs. Note that both cases result in inefficiency, since even after assuming that the aggregator perfectly follows the instruction, the costs can still differ, i.e., \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\mathbf L_i)\\ne c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"re The payoff of an aggregator i, deﬁned as πi, is calculated te, as the compensation pi received by","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The payoff of an aggregator \\(i\\), defined as \\(\\pi_i\\), is calculated as the compensation \\(p_i\\) received by the DSO for its flexibility services, minus the actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i\\) paid to its flexible customers according to \\(\\pi_i=p_i-\\tilde c_i\\quad (12)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the design of the reward function \\(p_i\\) we consider the family of mechanisms referred to as compensation and penalty mechanisms (CPM), as in [36, Sec. 10.6.1]. In our context, the aggregators’ instructions \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\), are calculated by solving problem (11), but using the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as the input that defines the functions \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\). After the observation of the outcome, the aggregator’s reward is calculated by \\(p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}\\quad (13)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions. Regarding the value of \\(\\gamma\\), the following theorem states that the proposed mechanism (14) is incentive compatible for \\(\\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: Given payment structure (14), for \\(\\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}\\), it is a dominant strategy for each aggregator to declare its true parameters \\((\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\mathbf L_i)\\) and then follow the DSO’s instructions \\((\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i=\\mathbf K_i^*)\\). Proof: The proof is provided in Appendix B."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E30","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"V2 ≤Vsqri,t ≤V2 ∀i ∈ b, t ∈ T (5) b 0 ≤Isqrij,t ≤I2ij ∀ij ∈ l, t ∈ T (6) der An aggregator’s ﬂexibil","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\mathbf K_i=\\{K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) denote \\(i\\)’s profile of flexibility activation. Accordingly, tuple \\(\\mathbf L_i=\\{\\lambda_{i,t},\\overline K_{i,t},\\underline K_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) denotes the aggregator’s local parameters, where \\(\\lambda_{i,t}\\) is a cost parameter for flexibility activation. It is assumed that the aggregator draws its flexibility by calling on demand-response resources from the prosumers in its portfolio at a cost defined by function \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E31","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p03-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL","anchor_text":"II. DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM MODEL We consider a distribution system, constituted by a set b of nodes b ∈","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We consider a distribution system, constituted by a set \\(\\Omega_b\\) of nodes \\(b\\in\\Omega_b\\) and a set \\(\\Omega_l\\) of lines \\(l\\in\\Omega_l\\). We consider a set of aggregators where each aggregator is responsible for the net demand of its portfolio. We assume that the DSO has access to measurement facilities and can observe each aggregator’s power consumption. In order to facilitate the analysis, we assume that each node of the distribution network is represented by one demand aggregator \\(i\\in\\Omega_b\\), however the methods and results of this article hold equivalently for the general case. For a horizon of discrete timeslots \\(t\\in\\Omega_T\\), an aggregator \\(i\\in\\Omega_b\\) is characterized by an active power demand profile \\(\\mathbf P^D_i=\\{P^D_{i,t},t\\in\\Omega_T\\}\\) constituted by the aggregator’s demand at each timeslot. The profile \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) represents the energy bought in the day-ahead market and it is communicated by the day-ahead market operator to the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E32","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p01-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"NOMENCLATURE","anchor_text":"NOMENCLATURE Sets A er b Set of nodes/aggregators. Fl l Set of lines. tow T Set of time periods. ati","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Sets: \\(\\Omega_b\\) Set of nodes/aggregators. \\(\\Omega_l\\) Set of lines. \\(\\Omega_T\\) Set of time periods. Parameters: \\(\\lambda_{i,t}\\) Parameter of flexibility cost at node i, period t [$/kWh^2]. \\(\\overline K_{i,t}\\) Maximum flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(\\underline K_{i,t}\\) Minimum flexibility factor at node i, period t. \\(\\overline I_{ij}\\) Maximum current magnitude for line ij [A]. \\(P^D_{i,t}\\) Active power demand at node i, period t [kW]. \\(Q^D_{i,t}\\) Reactive power demand at node i, period t [kvar]. \\(R_{ij}\\) Resistance of line ij [m\\Omega]. \\(\\overline V\\) Maximum voltage magnitude [kV]. \\(\\underline V\\) Minimum voltage magnitude [kV]. \\(X_{ij}\\) Reactance of line ij [m\\Omega]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_i = \\{\\hat{\\lambda}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline{K}}_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline{K}}_{i,t}\\} denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\mathbf{L}_i which may or may not be equal to the actual \\mathbf{L}_i. The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_i as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\mathbf{K}_i^* to follow."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}\\qquad (13)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j and instruction factors \\mathbf{K}_j^* of the rest of the aggregators, j\\neq i, but over the observed cost for i. Parameter \\beta is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: Given payment structure (14), for \\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}, it is a dominant strategy for each aggregator to declare its true parameters (\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_i=\\mathbf{L}_i) and then follow the DSO’s instructions (\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}}_i=\\mathbf{K}_i^*).\nProof: The proof is provided in Appendix B."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p09-c1r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"APPENDIX A","anchor_text":"APPENDIX A PROOF OF LEMMA 1 With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff πi from (12), considerin","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff \\pi_i from (12), considering K_i = K_i^* is given by \\pi_i = -\\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b,j\\neq i}\\left\\{c_j(K_j^*,\\hat L_j)\\right\\}\\right\\} \\tag{18}"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p10-c2r2__v141-a-M01-validation","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"APPENDIX B","anchor_text":"j∈ b:j̸=i n∈ b 9) Similarly, (33) can also be written as c∗i = max c∗n (37) 0) n∈ b For i to be bett","equation_id":"(39)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"With payment structure (14), the aggregator’s payoff is \\pi_i = R_i - \\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}\\left\\{c_j(K_j^*,\\hat L_j)\\right\\}\\right\\} - \\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2 \\tag{39}"}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-activation","v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing","v141-a-M01-settlement","v141-a-M01-validation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","quote":"The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","quote":"Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","quote":"The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","quote":"The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E18","quote":"The task of the DSO in this case is to design the reward function, \\(p_i\\),"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","quote":"Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","quote":"Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","quote":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","quote":"The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","quote":"The middle term is (13)"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","quote":"The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","quote":"This means that the penalization for deviations needs to be high enough so as to disincentivize misreporting, but at the same time not so high that aggregators with uncertainties would rather not participate in the market at all, in fear of negative payoffs and heavy penalties."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"d3112647a060e476a9f5f5d99254b1c2bf07c692c56c75cd7de6818af0f63c8e","applicability":"formalized","reason":"The settlement-payment rule is formalized from the supplied unreviewed source quotations: ECPM payment equation (14) at settlement.payment, its inherited CPM equation (13), the deviation-penalty component, and the stated gamma-dominant condition. Existing C.1 procedural constraints are retained unchanged. B evidence remains unreviewed; this is a provisional formalization, not an independent source audit.","bindings":[{"object_id":"var_beta","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"Beta is the DSO-chosen penalization factor in the compensation function, linking DSO design to settlement payment."},{"object_id":"var_gamma","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"Gamma is the DSO-adjustable deviation-penalty coefficient in the settlement payment rule."},{"object_id":"var_R_i","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"R_i is the fixed payment component designed within ECPM and paid at settlement."},{"object_id":"var_tildeK_i","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"The actual implemented flexibility profile is measured post-horizon and compared with the clearing instruction in the deviation penalty."},{"object_id":"par_K_i_star","node_ids":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"The clearing instruction K_i^* is an input to the settlement payment and to the deviation penalty."},{"object_id":"par_tilde_c_i","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"Observed actual cost and proof of payment enter the post-horizon compensation calculation."},{"object_id":"par_L_i","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"Actual local parameters underlie the actual flexibility cost used in the settlement payment."},{"object_id":"par_hatL_j","node_ids":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"Declared tuples of other aggregators are evaluated at their clearing instructions in the maximum-cost penalty."},{"object_id":"par_c_j_eval","node_ids":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"The declared cost of another aggregator evaluated at its instruction is the worst-off term in the payment rule."},{"object_id":"par_Omega_b","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"The set of aggregators indexes the maximum over j in the payment formula."},{"object_id":"par_Omega_T","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"The set of time periods indexes the quadratic deviation penalty sum."},{"object_id":"par_lambda_i_t","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"Lambda_i,t is the flexibility cost parameter compared with gamma in the stated incentive-compatibility condition."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","node_ids":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"Clearing instructions and measured outcomes feed the post-horizon settlement calculation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"The DSO designs the compensation rule and chooses beta, linking coordination to settlement."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"The fixed payment R_i is an ECPM settlement component intended to ensure participation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"Actual-cost reimbursement uses observed actual cost within the settlement payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001","v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"The deviation penalty compares implemented flexibility with the clearing instruction and is controlled by the DSO's gamma."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"Penalty selection is a DSO design trade-off affecting settlement participation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C007","node_ids":["clearing.local","coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001","v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"The ECPM formula combines clearing instructions, declared costs, measured actual outcomes, and DSO-chosen payment parameters at settlement."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C008","node_ids":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"The inherited CPM formula uses clearing instructions and declared costs in the settlement payment."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C009","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001","v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"The quadratic deviation-penalty component uses the clearing instruction and the DSO-chosen gamma at settlement."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C010","node_ids":["settlement.payment"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"The gamma-dominant condition is a DSO penalty-selection condition for the settlement payment rule."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
